<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Territory of Papua and New Guinea</h1><p class="st-meta">1949 CE – 1975 CE · capital Port Moresby</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The joint Australian administration of Papua and the trust territory of New Guinea, which led to Papua New Guinea's independence in 1975.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Australia</a> combined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-papua">Territory of Papua</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/territory-of-new-guinea">Territory of New Guinea</a> under the Papua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> Act of 1949, with a single administrator at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>. Legally, Papua remained an Australian territory and New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-hasluck">Paul Hasluck</a>, minister for territories from 1951 to 1963, pursued gradual uniform development through schools, health and patrol posts reaching the Highlands. A House of Assembly with an elected majority met from 1964. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a>, copra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panguna-mine">Panguna</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mine on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bougainville-island">Bougainville</a>, opened in 1972, drove the economy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangu-party">Pangu Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-somare">Michael Somare</a>, founded in 1967, pressed for self-government, achieved in 1973.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a> became independent on 16 September 1975, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-somare">Somare</a> as prime minister. The new state kept English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tok-pisin">Tok Pisin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiri-motu">Hiri Motu</a> as national languages and remained in the Commonwealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bougainville-island">Bougainville</a>'s grievances over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panguna-mine">Panguna mine</a> later fed secessionist war from 1988.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea.jpg" alt="Territory of Papua and New Guinea" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (Vector graphics image by Ian Fieggen) (only minor code changes by uploader.) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Australia_(converted).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1949 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Port Moresby</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Michael Somare</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Indigenous religions</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Tok Pisin, Hiri Motu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Papua and New Guinea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1962&amp;focus=territory-of-papua-and-new-guinea" aria-label="Territory of Papua and New Guinea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8741 2531 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Territory of Papua and New Guinea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M9061 2787L9061 2787M9061 2787L9221 2591M9061 2787L8774 3709"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M9061 2787h0M9221 2591h0M8774 3709h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9061 2787h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9061 2787h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Port Moresby. <a href="/a